too small; moving right down removes only sums that are too large.$$,$$The target, if it exists, remains achievable within the current pointer interval.$$,$$Moving both pointers on every iteration; using the unsorted-array proof; returning wrong 1-based indices.$$,$$Use long for overflow-prone sums. For many queries, consider preprocessing based on the query distribution.$$,$$public int[] twoSum(int[] a,int target){int l=0,r=a.length-1;while(l<r){long s=(long)a[l]+a[r];if(s==target)return new int[]{l+1,r+1};if(s<target)l++;else r--;}return new int[0];}$$,'CONTENT_REVIEW');\n\nINSERT INTO problem_followups(problem_id,question,type,sort_order) VALUES\n((SELECT id FROM problems WHERE slug='two-sum-ii'),$$Why can you discard an entire row/column of candidate pairs?$$,'FOLLOWUP',1),\n((SELECT id FROM problems WHERE slug='two-sum-ii'),$$Why are indices 1-based here?$$,'FOLLOWUP',2),\n((SELECT id FROM problems WHERE slug='two-sum-ii'),$$What if duplicates exist?$$,'FOLLOWUP',3),\n((SELECT id FROM problems WHERE slug='two-sum-ii'),$$How would you answer many target queries?$$,'SENIOR',4),\n((SELECT id FROM problems WHERE slug='two-sum-ii'),$$What if the input is only partially sorted?$$,'SENIOR',5)\nON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;\n\nINSERT INTO test_cases(id,problem_id,input,expected_output,is_hidden,display_order) VALUES\n(gen_random_uuid(),(SELECT id FROM problems WHERE slug='two-sum-ii'),$$4
